A CONTINUATION LINES UP UNDER THE ITEM IT CONTINUES, WHICHEVER ONE THAT IS.

Two items are split here, at two different depths, and each one's tail belongs
to a different item:

    Child tail   continues the CHILD, at level 1, so it lands at six columns
    Parent tail  continues the PARENT, at level 0, so it lands at two

Both tails sit at the same indent in the SOURCE. Only the item each one falls
inside decides where it is written, so a fix that remembered one indent for
the whole list, or that used the depth of the nested list rather than of the
item being continued, would put one of the two in the wrong column.

Four columns is the interesting wrong answer. Six is the content indent of a
level 1 item; a `Parent tail` written there would be two columns past its own
content start, and far enough past it on a deeper list to read back as an
indented code block. Prose would stop being prose.

`BlockFormatter` derives the column as indent + marker + one space, from the
item's own level and marker. That is the whole reason both come out right, and
this case is what says so.

The counts confirm it from the model's side: three items in, three out. The
two tails are continuations and neither is counted.
